WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.000
Before I do the exercise, I'm going to give you all a sneak peek into something that it is not heavily documented, this is an intermediate view thing, so we're going to talk about it and so it just leads a little bit more advanced.

00:11.000 --> 00:26.000
But what I wanted to show you before we moved on to the exercise is there is something inside of view, there is a new thing, at least I think it's new for a lot of people.

00:26.000 --> 00:29.000
It's called define expose.

00:29.000 --> 00:36.000
And what define expose does, actually let me just caution this, I can't put it warning enough on this.

00:36.000 --> 00:41.000
This is an advanced technique you should not be reaching for this.

00:41.000 --> 00:48.000
I wanted to show this because I do think people will find use for it, but this should not be your default thing to use, I cannot stress that enough.

00:48.000 --> 01:01.000
Okay, disclaimer is said, define expose everything I said about slots where I said like you know you're exposing it specifically to the slot define expose kind of like blows that up.

01:01.000 --> 01:16.000
And so it says in this particular case, let's say double count and then let's this can just be a method that then says current count.

01:16.000 --> 01:23.000
That you equals current count value times two easy enough.

01:23.000 --> 01:36.000
What it does is you give it an object and you say, hey, I want my parent to have access to these properties wherever.

01:36.000 --> 01:39.000
So you're thinking, okay, how does that work exactly?

01:39.000 --> 01:46.000
Well, the way it works is you need to first access the the child component itself.

01:46.000 --> 01:53.000
And so if you haven't done this before, this is known as a template ref so we can basically say a slot demo ref equals use template ref.

01:53.000 --> 02:01.000
And so what that does is it basically allows you to assign an ID to your component that basically allows you to fetch the DOM element you're actually picking up.

02:01.000 --> 02:08.000
So in this case, I'll just call it slot demo and then here we go just like that.

02:08.000 --> 02:21.000
Okay, and then once you have that, you can basically then say, if slot demo ref exists because when the component is initialized, it does not.

02:21.000 --> 02:30.000
You will notice that value.

02:31.000 --> 02:35.000
Everything is exposed or sorry, everything you define is exposed.

02:35.000 --> 02:39.000
So you like that thing where I mentioned that, oh, count is only stuck to the slot.

02:39.000 --> 02:46.000
Define expose says, actually we're going to let you expose it up here and you can play with it.

02:46.000 --> 02:57.000
Okay, so this is clearly kind of dangerous because it like it blows open the walls, right? You're like, oh man, no boundaries, this is amazing.

02:57.000 --> 03:01.000
You know, with with great freedom comes great responsibility.

03:01.000 --> 03:08.000
So why, so I remember when I first learned about this, my first slot was why the heck would I ever use scope slots?

03:08.000 --> 03:13.000
I'm just going to expose everything, right? That's dangerous for obvious reasons.

03:13.000 --> 03:19.000
The reason you scope things is because you wanted to be used within a certain context, that is still the advantage of a scope slot.

03:19.000 --> 03:23.000
And why I would say you really should be still using that as your default.

03:23.000 --> 03:33.000
The reason you might expose, however, in particular methods, and I reached out to the community and talked to a few people, some of them had like let's say a modal dialogue, right?

03:33.000 --> 03:35.000
A modal dialogue that would close itself.

03:35.000 --> 03:44.000
That's really not something you want to like have to like abstract out, and especially if you're generating a different close modal for each modal that you're generating.

03:44.000 --> 03:48.000
It's kind of weird to like abstract that out so that the parent also can get access to it.

03:48.000 --> 03:55.000
And so define expose is actually in this regard a very elegant way to say, hey look, you want to close this child's modal, right?

03:55.000 --> 03:58.000
So again, again, just for the sake of documenting.

03:58.000 --> 04:04.000
If there was a function called like close modal, and it would just, you know, set whatever.

04:04.000 --> 04:13.000
So cons is open equals ref true, and it's like, you know, is open dot value equals false.

04:14.000 --> 04:21.000
Like we already have this built into here, then there's really like, especially because a lot of times we are doing things programmatically, right?

04:21.000 --> 04:26.000
Like it's nice when you have a button that you collect that closes the modal, but a lot of times we might be chaining events together, right?

04:26.000 --> 04:31.000
If someone, if we know that the parent is doing something at the top, where then it eventually needs to close the child.

04:31.000 --> 04:40.000
Well, now you can actually then call that and just say, ref dot value, and you always need the optional, because remember that the component is not necessarily mounted at the initial part.

04:40.000 --> 04:48.000
And then at that point, then you can say close modal, and now you can programmatically invoke the child's method.

04:48.000 --> 04:50.000
Again, I can't stress this enough.

04:50.000 --> 04:56.000
Everyone I've talked to says, I struggle to find, use cases for this, but there, it does exist.

04:56.000 --> 04:58.000
There's reason this API exists.

04:58.000 --> 05:06.000
And so I want people to know about this because I think that's part of our role as developers is to understand these kind of techniques, which at face value,

05:06.000 --> 05:10.000
if using correctly, of course, can make a lot of spaghetti code, make our lives miserable.

05:10.000 --> 05:23.000
But we're learning to use the tradeoffs in more importantly, when we don't have a choice, right? I think someone else I talked to, they have third party UI libraries, where they have methods that are built into those UI libraries.

05:23.000 --> 05:31.000
It's kind of, if you, it's kind of redundant to like create a composable that refers to the components UI library that then you kind of like redirect.

05:31.000 --> 05:40.000
And so they just expose those methods directly through define expose, and then they're just leveraging the APIs directly.

05:40.000 --> 05:45.000
And so again, is this an edge case? Yes, that should first thing when you see this edge case.

05:45.000 --> 05:52.000
But define expose is the thing, and so, yeah, now that you know, now you know.

